Plugin Changelog
================

0.9

* Relay usable as the DNS server of the LAN, without Unbound or Dnsmasq on
  the firewall: several listen addresses, response cache (bounded by
  minimum TTL + TTL margin), allowed client networks (default: loopback and
  private networks, other sources dropped), rate limit per client.
* pf tables written by ioctl on /dev/pf (no process per answer), with
  automatic fallback to the pfctl command; backend shown in Settings.
* ASN sources: the prefixes announced in BGP by the ASNs of an application
  (RIPEstat, https) are added to its alias, refreshed daily and after Apply.

0.8

* Discovery removed (tab, API, settings, relay engine): it proposed names
  absent from the rules, which the relay and proactive resolution do not do:
  they only handle names already in the rules, plus their CNAME chains (names
  in the CNAME chain of an already covered name are still added by the relay).
  To complete an application, add the missing names by hand or from the
  catalog. Its stored data is deleted on upgrade. Rules that were ignored
  through Discovery no longer hold back catalog follow (Undo puts a rule
  into the declined list). keyword: and regexp: rules are kept but never
  applied.

0.7.1

* Applications grid: the rules column shows a short rule count with a
  tooltip instead of the full list; a single v2fly badge; a click on a row
  opens the editor; colors follow the theme (dark theme supported).

0.7

* Proactive resolution (on by default, also after an upgrade): the daemon
  itself resolves, at regular intervals, the names of the domain: and full:
  rules of the enabled applications (and, for domain: rules, the prefixes
  of the "Proactive prefixes" setting, www by default) to fill the pf
  aliases in advance, as a complement to the DNS relay. Settings: enable,
  minimum and maximum interval (300 and 3600 s by default), prefixes. Per
  application: "Proactive resolution" checkbox (badge in the grid). Names
  on shared platforms, regexp: and keyword: rules are never probed. 256
  names per application and 5000 in total at most. Counters (sent, ok,
  empty, failed, added) in the relay metrics.
* Model 0.6.0: proactive_enabled, proactive_interval_min,
  proactive_interval_max, proactive_prefixes and proactive per application
  (migration: enabled everywhere).
* Catalog, one gesture: adding an application is a name and an Add button,
  without checkboxes. The server computes the default selection (no shared
  platforms, no refused rules, ads excluded), the application follows the
  catalog by default, and the configuration is applied automatically.
* Online catalog check every hour by default (one API call per check; the
  archive is downloaded only when the list changed). daily and manual stay
  available; existing installations set to daily move to hourly on upgrade.
* Catalog follow applies a full export without reloading the firewall
  filter (no more alias sync), and applies nothing when the plugin is
  disabled (configuration saved, undoable).
* Shared platforms: dedicated sub-domains (youtubei.googleapis.com,
  yt3.googleusercontent.com) are included; whole parent domains of
  platforms (googleapis.com...) stay excluded; multi-tenant spaces
  (s3.amazonaws.com, blob.core.windows.net, storage.googleapis.com,
  github.io, workers.dev) cannot be taken as a whole.
* Warning "Catalog update paused" in the grid when a followed application
  would exceed 1000 rules.

0.6

* Catalog follow, per application: "Follow catalog automatically" (on for
  applications created or merged from the catalog, and for existing
  applications with catalog lists; off for manual applications). After each
  successful catalog update (daily, "Update catalog now", package upgrade
  with a newer embedded catalog), new rules of the catalog lists are added
  and rules removed from the catalog are removed, then applied without a
  click (export, alias sync, Unbound zones, relay reload), through the
  model and its validations. Rules added by hand are never changed; rules
  unchecked at import, shared platform names, keyword:/regexp: rules,
  ignored discovery rules and @ads rules excluded by the reference are
  never added. 1000 rules at most per application: beyond, nothing is
  applied and the event is logged. An application that fails is not saved
  and the error is logged; the others are.
* Journal of automatic changes (/var/db/appalias/catalog_follow.json, last
  200 changes) and one info log line per changed application. The Catalog
  tab shows the last 20 changes (date, application, added and removed rules)
  with an Undo button: added rules are removed and not added again, removed
  rules come back as rules added by hand, then the change is applied. Undo
  is refused when the application was deleted.
* "auto" badge next to the catalog lists in the grid; the manual catalog
  updates stay available for applications that do not follow the catalog.
* Discovery: a proposal whose CNAME chain goes through a name of the same
  application is "already covered" (the relay already adds its addresses):
  shown in a collapsed section, without Accept or Ignore, and no longer
  counted in the proposals nor in the grid badge.
* Layout of the Discovery and Catalog tabs reworked: aligned proposal rows
  (name and explanation, score and badges, rule and buttons), counters bar,
  Learn card, catalog cards of equal height, Popular apps gallery, relay
  badge; stacked under 768 px.
* Model 0.5.0: catalog_follow and catalog_declined per application
  (migration: existing applications with catalog lists follow the catalog,
  and the rules of their lists not imported so far are kept out).

0.5

* Discovery (off by default): the relay counts the DNS names it sees
  (names only, never which client asked) and suggests rules for the
  applications in a new Discovery tab: same CNAME target as a covered name,
  CNAME chain through a covered name, name of an imported v2fly list, names
  resolved together (co-occurrence), same site, keyword:/regexp: rules.
  Each proposal shows a score and its reasons; full: by default, domain:
  for groups of names (never on a shared platform). Accept, Ignore or
  Other app; nothing is applied before Apply. Learn mode: a 2 minute
  window that highlights the names seen while using an application.
  Retention (7 days by default), size cap, purge; ignored rules are kept in
  config.xml. Turning discovery off erases its data. On the answer path,
  observing a name is a single append to a bounded queue.
* Relay status at the top of the page: Running (PID, running version),
  Stopped or Config error, and a "restart needed" warning when the running
  code differs from the installed version.
* The note about forwarded domains bypassing DNS over TLS while the relay
  is down is informational while the relay runs, red only when it is
  stopped.
* Optional online catalog update (off by default): "Update catalog now" in
  the Catalog tab, or daily, downloads the latest
  v2fly/domain-list-community from GitHub (HTTPS, api.github.com and
  codeload.github.com only, 20 MB cap). Extraction and checks run as an
  unprivileged user; the downloaded catalog replaces the embedded one only
  if it is newer and passes the checks (500 lists at least, popular lists
  present, no broken list). "Revert to embedded" deletes it. New rules
  still go through the catalog updates to review.
* Model 0.4.0: discovery and catalog download settings.

0.4.2

* Upgrade: the relay is restarted after the package is upgraded when it is
  running, so the new code is used right away (a reload only rereads the
  configuration).

0.4.1

* Settings: choosing DNS over TLS sets the upstream port to 853 (when it is
  53 or empty) and fills in the TLS name for Quad9, Cloudflare and Google
  public resolvers; going back to plain DNS puts 853 back to 53. A custom
  port is never changed.
* Validation: DNS over TLS with port 53 and plain DNS with port 853 are
  refused when saving, for the main upstream and for fallback upstreams with
  an explicit port. A configuration already saved that way (0.4) still
  starts: the relay corrects the port (53 to 853 with DNS over TLS, 853 to
  53 with plain DNS) and logs one warning per correction.

0.4

* DNS over TLS (RFC 7858) to the upstream: new Upstream protocol (plain
  DNS by default) and Upstream TLS name settings. The certificate and its
  name are always checked (system CA bundle, TLS 1.2 at least) and the relay
  never falls back to plain DNS: on failure it tries the next fallback
  upstream, then answers SERVFAIL. Persistent connections per upstream (one
  query at a time per connection, bounded pool, 30 s idle timeout). An
  answer larger than the UDP size of the client is returned truncated
  (TC=1) so that the client retries over TCP. TLS failures are logged as
  warnings, rate limited. Example: 9.9.9.9, port 853, dns.quad9.net.
* Fallback upstreams: ip, ip:port, [ipv6] and [ipv6]:port, with an optional
  #tls-name suffix; default port 53 (plain DNS) or 853 (DNS over TLS). An
  address without port (149.112.112.112) is no longer refused.
* Latency measurements: one info log line per answer that adds addresses
  (upstream and pf time), average and p95 per upstream and for pf over the
  last 256 measurements in the status and in Settings.
* Unbound forwarding: the loop check also covers port 853 and the relay
  port, whatever the upstream protocol; "Use System Nameservers" is
  detected as a "." forward.
* Catalog updates: refused new rules are shown as a warning, not an error.
* Model 0.3.0: upstream_protocol and upstream_tls_name.

0.3

* Catalog tab: search, preview and import of the lists of an embedded
  snapshot of v2fly/domain-list-community (pinned commit, sha256 checked,
  category-*, geolocation-*, tld-*, cn, private and "!" lists excluded).
  Rules tagged @ads and rules on shared platforms are unchecked by default.
  Nothing is applied before "Apply".
* Catalog updates: imported applications keep their catalog references and
  show the rules added to or removed from the catalog, to review and accept.
* Shared platform guard (CDN, cloud, hosting): a domain: rule on a whole
  shared platform, on one of its parents or on a top-level domain alone is
  refused by the model and by the relay; extra names can be configured.
* keyword: and regexp: are discovery rules: kept, never applied by the
  relay, with a single debug log line instead of a warning on each reload.
* Automatic Unbound forwarding (opt-in): one forward zone per application
  domain to the relay, with forward-first, in
  /usr/local/etc/unbound.opnsense.d/appalias.conf. While the relay is down,
  these domains fall back to direct recursion (plain DNS, port 53),
  bypassing DNS over TLS and the "." Query Forwarding; with outgoing port 53
  blocked they fail until the relay is back. Apply restarts Unbound
  only when the list of forwarded domains changes. Names already configured
  in Unbound (Query Forwarding, DNS over TLS, domain overrides), their
  sub-domains, local names and children of a forwarded parent are skipped
  and listed in Settings; no zone is written when the relay upstream is the
  firewall itself (loop). Other queries keep their path (DNS over TLS).
* Model 0.2.0: hidden catalog fields per application, Origin field removed
  from the application dialog.
* Source texts in English (translations are handled by the OPNsense
  translation project).

0.2

* Install migration fix: upstream_address is no longer required by the
  model (check moved to the validation, required only when the service is
  enabled).
* New Log page (Services > AppAlias > Log File).
* Reconfigure: the result of each configd call is checked (template,
  export, synchronization, start, reload).
* Status: widget captions (Restart, Start, Stop).
* Alias synchronization and re-creation of missing aliases under the
  config.xml lock.
* System boot: no more double start of the daemon, the bootup event exports
  the configuration and reloads the daemon if it is already running.
* Grid error tooltip escaped (jQuery construction); the grid is no longer
  reloaded twice when the page loads.
* sessionClose() removed from ServiceController (not needed with the 26.7
  core, the session is released at construction).

0.1

* First version (MVP): DNS relay, domain:/full: rules, manual CIDRs.
